The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and provides a ready-made `IFileSystem` interface covering all three calls.

**1. Install the packages:**

```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**

```csharp
public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
// Directory.Exists will return true for "/"
var service = new ConfigService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Load("/config.json"));

service.Save("/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/out.txt"));
```

**Why this over a custom wrapper?**

| Approach | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Custom `IFileWrapper` | You'd only cover the 3 methods you use today; grows over time |
| `System.IO.Abstractions` | Covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream`, etc. — complete and maintained |

If your project **doesn't use DI**, you can still inject `IFileSystem` via constructor parameters and pass `new FileSystem()` at the composition root. No container required.

